  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}+xy-4{z}^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =3(tx)^{2}+(tx)(ty)-4(tz)^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy-4{t}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+xy-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{2}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $2.$",
